<h2>Why the Act singles out institutions</h2>
<p>Most of the POCSO Act is written for everyone. Three parts of its scheme, however, are aimed squarely at those who run places where children study, live or train: the mandatory reporting provisions in Sections 19 to 21, the aggravated-offence categories in Sections 5 and 9 that turn on institutional position, and the preventive obligations that the POCSO Rules, 2020 place on establishments in regular contact with children. Read together, they convert what managements often treat as a reputational problem into a defined set of legal duties — with personal criminal consequences for the person in charge who ignores them.</p>

<h2>The reporting duty as it applies to management</h2>
<p>Section 19 obliges <strong>any person</strong> — a teacher, warden, coach, counsellor, accountant or director — who has knowledge that an offence under the Act has been committed, or even an <em>apprehension that it is likely to be committed</em>, to give that information to the Special Juvenile Police Unit (SJPU) or the local police. The duty is individual: it does not travel up a chain of command, and informing one’s own principal or HR department does not discharge it. Section 20 adds a parallel duty for personnel of media, hotels, hospitals, clubs, studios and photographic facilities who come across material that is sexually exploitative of a child.</p>
<p>Section 21 supplies the sanction. Failure to report or record an offence is punishable with imprisonment up to six months, or fine, or both. The provision then does something unusual: it creates a distinct, heavier offence for institutional heads. A <strong>person in charge of a company or an institution</strong> who fails to report the commission of an offence by a <strong>subordinate under his control</strong> faces imprisonment up to one year and fine. A principal who learns of a credible allegation against a teacher and chooses silence is, therefore, not merely negligent — the statute makes the silence itself an offence. The Act shields children from this penalty, and it protects informants who report in good faith from civil and criminal liability, so fear of a defamation claim is no answer to the duty.</p>

<h2>Staff offences are aggravated offences</h2>
<p>Where the alleged offender is on the institution’s rolls, the charge itself changes. Sections 5 and 9 list the circumstances in which penetrative sexual assault and sexual assault become their aggravated forms, and the list prominently includes offences committed by the <strong>management or staff of an educational institution or religious institution</strong>, of a hospital, jail, remand home or protection home, on a child in that institution, as well as offences by any person in a <strong>position of trust or authority</strong> over the child. For institutions the practical message is twofold: the sentencing exposure of staff is far higher than for strangers, and the accused’s institutional role becomes a fact in issue at trial — appointment records, duty rosters and supervision arrangements will be summoned and scrutinised.</p>

<h2>Prevention: recruitment, supervision and policy</h2>
<p>The POCSO Rules, 2020 carry the Act’s scheme into the pre-offence stage. For institutions that house children or are in regular contact with them — schools, crèches, sports academies and similar facilities — the Rules contemplate periodic police verification and background checks of staff, regular orientation and training of employees on child safety, and the adoption of a child protection policy consistent with the government’s child protection framework. Even where a particular establishment debates whether it falls within the Rules’ letter, these requirements are the natural benchmark against which an institution’s diligence will later be judged, and prudent managements treat them as a floor: documented verification before hiring, defined norms on staff–student contact (including online contact), and a named officer responsible for receiving complaints.</p>

<h2>The child’s side: CWC and the Juvenile Justice framework</h2>
<p>Once a report is made, the institution does not control what happens to the child. Under Section 19, where the SJPU or police are satisfied that the child needs care and protection, the matter must be placed before the <strong>Child Welfare Committee</strong> constituted under the Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act, 2015. The Committee assesses whether the child can safely remain where she is — a live question when the child resides in the very hostel or child-care institution where the offence allegedly occurred — and may direct interim care arrangements. Under the POCSO Rules, 2020 the Committee can also provide a support person to assist the child through investigation and trial. Management’s role is cooperation: producing records, facilitating access, and never pressuring the child or family.</p>

<h2>When a complaint surfaces: the sequence</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><h4>1. Report to SJPU or local police</h4><p>Immediately, under Section 19 — not after an internal committee “concludes”. Note who reported, when and to whom.</p></div>
<div class="fstep"><h4>2. Secure the child</h4><p>Separate the child from the person complained against; arrange support through a parent or trusted adult; cooperate with the CWC’s directions on care.</p></div>
<div class="fstep"><h4>3. Preserve everything</h4><p>Attendance registers, CCTV footage, duty rosters, staff files, devices and correspondence. Destruction or alteration of material invites prosecution for causing evidence to disappear under the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023.</p></div>
<div class="fstep"><h4>4. Suspend contact, not the law</h4><p>Administrative steps against the staff member may proceed, but they supplement — never replace — the criminal process.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>What management must not do</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
<li>No internal “settlement”, apology meeting or compensation in lieu of a report — POCSO offences are not private disputes, and brokering silence compounds the Section 21 exposure.</li>
<li>No disclosure of the child’s identity to staff, parents’ groups or the press — Section 23 POCSO penalises identity disclosure.</li>
<li>No quiet transfer or resignation of the accused staffer as a substitute for reporting.</li>
<li>No questioning of the child by management panels; the statutory scheme entrusts the child’s statement to the police and Magistrate under child-sensitive procedures.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>The pattern in institutional POCSO matters is consistent: the original allegation is investigated by the police, but the management’s conduct in the first forty-eight hours is what generates the second file — the Section 21 complaint against the person in charge. A reporting protocol that every employee knows, verified hiring, and an instinct to preserve rather than manage, protect the child first and the institution with it.</p>
</div>
